{"parentUuid":"571c8094-dc0c-45f7-89e4-89f9232c8f72","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CftJXoxtXq8hcXvcmUjyn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RZ48A9vmnxdCY1EtCXUQUc","name":"mcp__Claude_Browser__javascript_tool","input":{"action":"javascript_exec","text":"const h=[...document.querySelectorAll('h2,h3')].find(x=>/FAQs/.test(x.textContent)); const sec=h.closest('section')||h.parentElement.parentElement; const qs=[...sec.querySelectorAll('button,[aria-expanded],summary')]; const out=[]; for (const q of qs){ q.click(); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,350)); out.push(sec.innerText); q.click(); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,200)); } const base=out.map(t=>t); const uniq=new Set(); const lines=[]; for (const t of base){ for (const l of t.split('\\n')){ if(l.length>60 && !uniq.has(l)){uniq.add(l); lines.push(l);} } } lines.join('\\n\\n').slice(0,6000)","tabId":"seed"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":740826,"output_tokens":443,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":49},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":725,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":443,"cache_read_input_tokens":740826,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":725},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RZ48A9vmnxdCY1EtCXUQUc":{"action":"javascript_exec","text":"const h=[...document.querySelectorAll('h2,h3')].find(x=>/FAQs/.test(x.textContent)); const sec=h.closest('section')||h.parentElement.parentElement; const qs=[...sec.querySelectorAll('button,[aria-expanded],summary')]; const out=[]; for (const q of qs){ q.click(); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,350)); out.push(sec.innerText); q.click(); await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,200)); } const base=out.map(t=>t); const uniq=new Set(); const lines=[]; for (const t of base){ for (const l of t.split('\\n')){ if(l.length>60 && !uniq.has(l)){uniq.add(l); lines.push(l);} } } lines.join('\\n\\n').slice(0,6000)","tabId":"seed"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CftJXoZ5e4FQdNZ3UdNGb","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionMcpServer":"Claude Browser","attributionMcpTool":"javascript_tool","type":"assistant","uuid":"a929395a-49e6-4326-82a5-932d156536ad","timestamp":"2026-10-10T09:15:41.851Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"F:\\Users\\shrey\\Desktop\\AI Project\\backend","sessionId":"fd077be5-cd09-4d91-beb4-30df27c1398e","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"fluttering-mapping-sloth"}
{"parentUuid":"a929395a-49e6-4326-82a5-932d156536ad","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cde90b88-60f3-4a8e-aba6-b51e470ae5ea","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RZ48A9vmnxdCY1EtCXUQUc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"neatHack is a 48-hour online hackathon on October 10–12, 2026, starting Saturday at 12:30 PM IST. The kickoff call is at 9:00 AM IST on October 10. Submissions open October 12 at 7:00 PM IST and close at 11:55 PM IST. Winners are announced on October 16.\\n\\nCome solo or in a team of 1–4 people. Every member must register on Luma using the same team name, and each person can join only one team.\\n\\nYou can plan your idea in advance, but all code must be written between the start on October 10 and the deadline on October 12. Finished or half-built projects cannot be brought into the event. Use neatlogs throughout development to capture traces, investigate failures, identify bottlenecks and improve your agent.\\n\\nThere is one track, Build an agent that works (literally), and every project competes for the same prizes. You can build a single agent or a system of several agents. The example tasks are ideas, not separate tracks.\\n\\nYes. Every project must use neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai, with a clear role for each in building the agent or planning its business. Your demo must cover all three tools.\\n\\nEach team submits one project as a public quote post of the official neatlogs submission post on X before the deadline. Use Submit on X to open the draft, fill in your team, name, project, public repository and demo link, keep the official post attached, then publish on X. Include supporting evidence in your repository. Include a demo video of three minutes or less and a public code repository. The video must explicitly cover neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai: architecture, implementation, runtime, reasoning and result for each tool. Judges must be able to verify that commits were made during the hackathon. Late submissions will not be judged. Submissions are open on October 12 from 7:00 PM to 11:55 PM IST. The Submit on X button appears during that window. Opening the draft alone does not submit your project. neatlogs and cfo.ai will engage with the first 50 quote tweets.\\n\\nMentors score each project using the published criteria. The agent is worth 30% of the final score, the use of neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai is worth 25%, and the demo video is worth 20%. Building in public is worth 15%, and usefulness and originality are worth 10%. The judges' decision is final, and the community winner is judged separately.\\n\\nThe community winner goes to the project that generates the strongest genuine community interest during the hackathon. We look at the quality and creativity of the product and demo, the quality of the launch and content, shares and discussion, genuine engagement, and whether people are excited enough to try, share or talk about it. Existing audience size or follower count does not decide the winner. The project must use neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai, but partner usage is not part of community winner scoring. Bought likes or bot activity will disqualify a team.\\n\\nBe respectful in Discord and all event spaces. Harassment, spam or plagiarism will result in removal from the hackathon.\\n\\nNo. Bedrock isn’t included in the hackathon AWS accounts. The $25 allowance per team or solo covers only the approved AWS services. Use your own OpenAI/Anthropic API keys for model calls; those providers bill usage separately. Your AWS access email includes the permitted services and usage rules.\\n\\nJoin neatlogs.com/discord for official neatHack communication. AWS credits go to the first 350 teams to complete web check-in. Crustdata gives 5,000 credits to everyone participating. Dodo Payments credits are for the top three winners only: $5,000 for first, $3,000 for second and $1,000 for third. Each winner gets 3 months of neatlogs Pro (worth $1500). Access and redemption details will be published with the resources. Workshops and office hours run on both days.\"\n\n(captured at origin https://neatlogs.com)"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nTab Context:\n- Executed on tabId: seed\n- Available tabs:\n  • tabId seed: \"neatHack — AI agent hackathon | neatlogs\" (\"https://neatlogs.com/hackathon\")"}]}]},"uuid":"e9bc559b-718b-4985-b70d-9ce482a4a4d6","timestamp":"2026-10-10T09:15:50.642Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"\"neatHack is a 48-hour online hackathon on October 10–12, 2026, starting Saturday at 12:30 PM IST. The kickoff call is at 9:00 AM IST on October 10. Submissions open October 12 at 7:00 PM IST and close at 11:55 PM IST. Winners are announced on October 16.\\n\\nCome solo or in a team of 1–4 people. Every member must register on Luma using the same team name, and each person can join only one team.\\n\\nYou can plan your idea in advance, but all code must be written between the start on October 10 and the deadline on October 12. Finished or half-built projects cannot be brought into the event. Use neatlogs throughout development to capture traces, investigate failures, identify bottlenecks and improve your agent.\\n\\nThere is one track, Build an agent that works (literally), and every project competes for the same prizes. You can build a single agent or a system of several agents. The example tasks are ideas, not separate tracks.\\n\\nYes. Every project must use neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai, with a clear role for each in building the agent or planning its business. Your demo must cover all three tools.\\n\\nEach team submits one project as a public quote post of the official neatlogs submission post on X before the deadline. Use Submit on X to open the draft, fill in your team, name, project, public repository and demo link, keep the official post attached, then publish on X. Include supporting evidence in your repository. Include a demo video of three minutes or less and a public code repository. The video must explicitly cover neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai: architecture, implementation, runtime, reasoning and result for each tool. Judges must be able to verify that commits were made during the hackathon. Late submissions will not be judged. Submissions are open on October 12 from 7:00 PM to 11:55 PM IST. The Submit on X button appears during that window. Opening the draft alone does not submit your project. neatlogs and cfo.ai will engage with the first 50 quote tweets.\\n\\nMentors score each project using the published criteria. The agent is worth 30% of the final score, the use of neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai is worth 25%, and the demo video is worth 20%. Building in public is worth 15%, and usefulness and originality are worth 10%. The judges' decision is final, and the community winner is judged separately.\\n\\nThe community winner goes to the project that generates the strongest genuine community interest during the hackathon. We look at the quality and creativity of the product and demo, the quality of the launch and content, shares and discussion, genuine engagement, and whether people are excited enough to try, share or talk about it. Existing audience size or follower count does not decide the winner. The project must use neatlogs, Entire and cfo.ai, but partner usage is not part of community winner scoring. Bought likes or bot activity will disqualify a team.\\n\\nBe respectful in Discord and all event spaces. Harassment, spam or plagiarism will result in removal from the hackathon.\\n\\nNo. Bedrock isn’t included in the hackathon AWS accounts. The $25 allowance per team or solo covers only the approved AWS services. Use your own OpenAI/Anthropic API keys for model calls; those providers bill usage separately. Your AWS access email includes the permitted services and usage rules.\\n\\nJoin neatlogs.com/discord for official neatHack communication. AWS credits go to the first 350 teams to complete web check-in. Crustdata gives 5,000 credits to everyone participating. Dodo Payments credits are for the top three winners only: $5,000 for first, $3,000 for second and $1,000 for third. Each winner gets 3 months of neatlogs Pro (worth $1500). Access and redemption details will be published with the resources. Workshops and office hours run on both days.\"\n\n(captured at origin https://neatlogs.com)"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nTab Context:\n- Executed on tabId: seed\n- Available tabs:\n  • tabId seed: \"neatHack — AI agent hackathon | neatlogs\" (\"https://neatlogs.com/hackathon\")"}],"classifierBoundary":true,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a929395a-49e6-4326-82a5-932d156536ad","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"F:\\Users\\shrey\\Desktop\\AI Project\\backend","sessionId":"fd077be5-cd09-4d91-beb4-30df27c1398e","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main","slug":"fluttering-mapping-sloth"}
